\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Server-side hash latency by algorithm across payload sizes (\texttt {sign\_only} scenario, warm, median with 95\% CI bands). BLAKE3 and SHA-256 are nearly indistinguishable at small sizes; Keccak-256 diverges progressively beyond 100\nobreakspace {}KB.}}{36}{figure.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Server-side signing latency by profile across payload sizes (SHA-256 hash, \texttt {sign\_only} scenario, warm, median). Note the logarithmic scale: SLH-DSA is separated from all other profiles by three orders of magnitude. All remaining profiles cluster between 0.18\nobreakspace {}ms and 5.3\nobreakspace {}ms.}}{38}{figure.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Server-side verification latency by profile (SHA-256 hash, \texttt {verify\_manifest} scenario, warm, median with 95\% CI bands). FN-DSA achieves the fastest public-key verification in the suite.}}{40}{figure.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Signature size by algorithm on a linear scale (median bytes per condition). The PQC schemes span two orders of magnitude, from FN-DSA's 654\nobreakspace {}bytes to SLH-DSA's 7\,854\nobreakspace {}bytes.}}{41}{figure.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Storage amplification ratio (total stored bytes / raw file bytes) by profile and payload size. PQC overhead is most pronounced for small payloads; FN-DSA remains competitive with RSA-PSS across the full size range.}}{42}{figure.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces End-to-end throughput (MiB/s) by profile across payload sizes. All non-SLH-DSA profiles produce nearly identical throughput curves; SLH-DSA is separated by two to three orders of magnitude at small sizes.}}{44}{figure.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Server-side overhead ratio relative to RSA-PSS baseline with bootstrapped 95\% confidence intervals (\texttt {sign\_only} scenario). Ratios below 1.0 indicate lower overhead than RSA-PSS. FN-DSA, ML-DSA, EdDSA, and ECDSA are all below the RSA-PSS reference.}}{45}{figure.7}%
